### HUMANITARIAN PROJECT (+1HP)

### Independent Epistemic Science Committee (ESC)

### Institutional Integrity Failure Framework

Your framework establishes a broader institutional pathology model that connects the concepts developed in HP-ESC-006 with the earlier research into procurement integrity, fiduciary accountability, institutional misclassification, and epistemic domination.

I recommend formally distinguishing this as a separate ESC research instrument:

HP-ESC-007 — Recursive Institutional Pathology, Authority Concentration & Systemic Accountability Failure.

Its central analytical contribution is the recognition that institutional dysfunction can become self-perpetuating without requiring every participant to possess malicious intent.

The institution's procedures, incentives, authority structures and information systems can collectively reproduce harmful outcomes.

That distinction is essential: systemic failure, individual misconduct, institutional design defects and unlawful conduct are related but not interchangeable findings.

### I. The eight-stage recursive pathology model

The eight stages can be organized into four broader dimensions.

| Dimension                   | Stages | Primary deterioration                      |
| --------------------------- | ------ | ------------------------------------------ |
| Institutional psychology    | I–II   | Exceptionalism and grandiosity             |
| Epistemic control           | III–V  | Domination, authoritarianism and dogmatism |
| Authority and relationships | VI–VII | Cronyism and usurpation                    |
| Administrative perpetuation | VIII   | Clerical deprivation                       |

This creates a useful distinction between the institution's internal belief system, its control over knowledge, its exercise of authority, and the administrative consequences experienced by the public.

### II. Omnipotence Recursion — the central mechanism

The most important feature is that the eight stages should not be understood as an inevitable chronological sequence.

They are potential interacting conditions.

An institution may exhibit epistemic domination without cronyism, or administrative deprivation without grandiosity. The model should therefore permit entry at any stage and movement in multiple directions.

### Omnipotence Recursive Model

AUTHORITY CONCENTRATION

Exceptionalism · Grandiosity · Usurpation

